\ ff64.help — help for words new or different in the x86-64 port
\ Words that also appear in ff.help are omitted here unless their
\ x86-64 behavior is materially different.  Use "help <word>" to
\ search both files.
\ Format: entry lines start at column 0; continuation lines start
\ with two spaces.  Related words form a block; "see also" lists the
\ block head first so "help <head>" shows the whole group.

\ === x86-64 data sizes ===

d@ ( addr -- n ) fetch 64-bit (double cell) value
  On x86-64, @ already fetches 64 bits, so d@ is equivalent to @.
  Provided for clarity when 64-bit intent matters.
d! ( n addr -- ) store 64-bit (double cell) value
  On x86-64, ! already stores 64 bits, so d! is equivalent to !.
d, ( n -- ) compile a 64-bit value at here; advance here by 8
  On i386, , compiles 32 bits. On x86-64, , compiles 32 bits (for opcodes).
  d, compiles a full 64-bit value (addresses, pointers).
w, ( n -- ) compile a 16-bit value at here; advance here by 2
  Useful for emitting 16-bit opcode fragments.
d@` d!` d,` 2dupd!` tuckd!` compile-time macros for the above
  see also: d@ d! d, w, @ ! ,

8+` compile-time macro: add 8 to TOS (one cell on x86-64)
  The x86-64 equivalent of i386's 4+` (cell+).
8-` compile-time macro: subtract 8 from TOS
  see also: 8+` 4+` 4-`

\ === Compile-time stack ===

>cs ( n -- ) push a value onto the compile-time stack
  Used by START/ENTER/BREAK/END for forward-reference resolution.
cs> ( -- n ) pop a value from the compile-time stack
  see also: >cs

\ === Dictionary / headers ===

h.name ( hdr -- ) print the name field of a dictionary header

h.next ( hdr -- hdr' ) advance to the next dictionary header
  Computes hdr + name length + fixed header overhead.
  see also: h.name h.next

\ === Vector system ===

:^ ( <name> -- ) define a vector (redirectable word)
  Creates a word with a push/ret preamble: 68 <target32> C3.
  The body follows at xt+6.  The push operand can be rewritten
  by !^ n^ ^^ to redirect or disable the vector.
!^` ( val vec !^ -- ) compile: set vector target from TOS
  Backtick macro.  Uncalls preceding word to get xt, compiles
  code that stores TOS into the vector's push operand at xt+1.
@^` ( vec @^ -- val ) compile: read current vector target
  Backtick macro.  Uncalls preceding word, compiles inline d@
  that reads the 32-bit push operand at xt+1.
n^` ( vec n^ -- ) compile: nop a vector (make it do nothing)
  Backtick macro.  Uncalls preceding word, stores _nop's xt
  into the push operand so the vector does nothing when called.
x^` ( vec x^ -- ) compile: execute a vector's body directly
  Backtick macro.  Uncalls preceding word, compiles code that
  pushes xt+6 onto the return stack, skipping the push/ret.
^^` ( vec ^^ -- ) compile: reset vector to its default body
  Backtick macro.  Uncalls preceding word, compiles code that
  writes xt+6 (body start) into the push operand.
  see also: :^ !^ @^ n^ x^ ^^

\ === Peephole optimizers ===

++` compile-time macro: increment variable in place
  Rewrites a preceding variable push into an in-place increment.
  Usage: variable x  ... x ++
--` compile-time macro: decrement variable in place
  see also: ++` --`

\ === Control flow additions ===

ds ( -- ) display the data stack contents (runtime)
  .s` is the compile-time equivalent (used in prompts).

ss ( -- ) display stack as ( depth; val1 val2 ... )
  Shows depth count and each value in hex.

dd ( -- ) drain data stack: drop all values

cond.` compile-time macro: convert stack boolean to FLAGS
  Emits: 0- drop 0<>
  Used by IF. WHILE. UNTIL. (dotted conditionals) to bridge
  stack booleans to FreeForth's FLAGS-based control flow.

LOOP` compile-time macro: close a TIMES counted loop
  Decrements counter, branches back if nonzero.
  see also: TIMES REPEAT

C1?` compile-time macro: test CPU carry flag (set)
  Stores JB ($72) in cond_jmp. Use after add/adc/sbb
  to branch on overflow/borrow.
  Example: $FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF 1 + C1? IF ( carry set ) THEN

C0?` compile-time macro: test CPU carry flag (clear)
  Stores JAE ($73) in cond_jmp. Complement of C1?.
  Example: 5 1 + C0? IF ( no carry ) THEN

\ === Boot infrastructure ===

loadfile ( addr len -- ) load and compile a Forth source file
  Opens the named file, reads it into filebuf, passes contents
  through the compiler.  Saves and restores parsing state.

ff_argc ( -- addr ) variable: command-line argument count
ff_argv ( -- addr ) variable: pointer to argv array
  Set by assembly at startup.
  see also: ff_argc ff_argv argv doargv

helpbuf ( -- addr ) 128KB buffer used by the help system
  Used by lib/64/help.ff to read ff.help into memory.

xfp ( -- addr ) variable: exception frame pointer for catch/throw
  Points to the current catch frame on the return stack.
  Zero when no catch frame is active.

stderr ( -- 2 ) file descriptor constant for standard error

features ( -- addr ) buffer holding loaded feature names
  Space-separated list of features.  Printed by -v.
-v ( -- ) display loaded features
  see also: features -v

[os] ( -- 1 ) compile-time constant: operating system flag
  Returns 1 for Linux.

cell` ( -- 8 ) compile-time constant: cell size in bytes
  8 on ff64 (x86-64), 4 on ff (i386).
  Use for portable address arithmetic: cell allot, cell +, etc.
  see also: [64] cell+ cells

[64]` ( -- 4 | 0 ) compile-time constant: 64-bit architecture flag
  Returns 4 (truthy) on ff64, 0 (falsy) on ff.
  Derived from cell: cell 4 -
  Use with [IF] for conditional compilation:
    [64] [IF] \ 64-bit code [ELSE] \ 32-bit code [THEN]
  see also: cell [IF] [ELSE] [THEN]

pad ( -- addr ) scratch buffer: here + 256
  Temporary workspace safe from ongoing compilation at here.
  Address changes whenever here changes — don't hold across defs.

nop` ( -- ) compile a NOP (0x90) machine instruction
  : nop` $90 c, ;

rp@` ( -- rsp ) compile: push the return stack pointer
  On ff64, pushes RSP (the x86-64 call stack pointer).
  : rp@` over` $48, ,1 $E389, s01 ;

sp@` ( -- sp ) compile: push the data stack pointer
  On ff64, pushes R15 (the memory stack base pointer).
  Note: R15 is the memory portion of the register+memory stack.
  The top 9 stack items live in registers, not at [R15].
  : sp@` over` $4C, ,1 $FB89, s01 ;

quit ( -- ) reset _top to default and restart the REPL

\ === File loading ===

needexec ( addr len -- ) load a file and execute its last definition
  Calls needed (with double-load guard), then executes the most
  recently defined word via H@ @ execute.
  see also: needed needs` needexec loadfile openlib -f`

\ === Signal handling ===

SEGVhndlr ( -- ) signal handler for SIGSEGV
  Throws "SEGV caught" via !" (inline error + throw).
  Installed via sigaction during boot.
SEGVthrow ( -- ) install SEGV handler via sigaction
  Called automatically during boot.  Can re-install after fork.
  see also: SEGVhndlr SEGVthrow catch throw

\ === Library words (load with "needs <file>") ===

fixup ( addr len -- ) self-patching libc symbol resolver
  Used in hidden definitions: :. _xxx "symbol" fixup ;
  On first call: resolves via dlsym, builds a trampoline
  (movabs rbx,imm64; ret), patches caller's call instruction.
  Subsequent calls go directly to the trampoline.
  IMPORTANT: the hidden definition MUST end with ; for tail-call.
  Loaded from: "fixup.ff" needed
  see also: fixup #lib #fun #call libc@

strerror ( errno -- ) print error message for errno
  Takes a negative errno, negates, calls C strerror() via fixup.
  Example: -13 strerror  →  "Permission denied"
ior ( -- addr ) variable: last I/O result
?ior ( n -- n ) store I/O result in ior; keep n on stack
?ior. ( n -- n ) store in ior; if error, print message and throw
  Loaded from: "ior.ff" needed
  see also: strerror ior ?ior ?ior.

cls ( -- ) clear screen (ANSI escape)
home ( -- ) move cursor to 0,0
atxy ( col row -- ) position cursor
atx ( col -- ) position cursor on current row
color ( n -- ) set ANSI color/attribute
key? ( -- ; zFALSE ) test if key is available on stdin
ekey ( -- c ) read one key in raw mode
  Loaded from: "console.ff" needed
  see also: cls home atxy atx color key? ekey

.now ( -- ) display current date and time
  Loaded from: "time.ff" needed
  see also: .now .d .wd .dt .t ms@ ms

\ === Number literal formats ===

$N hex literal: $FF = 255, $DEADBEEF
&N octal literal: &100 = 64, &777 = 511
%N binary literal: %1010 = 10
N#M explicit base: 8#77 = 63, 16#ff = 255
'C ASCII literal: 'A = 65, '0 = 48
N'N digit grouping (skip chars): 1'000'000 = 1000000
  Apostrophe, comma, period, and slash are skip characters.
Y-M-D Gregorian date: 2000-3-1 = 730485
H:M:S time (x60 accumulation): 1:0:0 = 3600
D_H:M:S day-hour-minute-second: 1_0:0:0 = 86400
  see also: $N &N %N

\ === Syscall interface (fflin64.boot) ===

syscall ( args... #args syscall# -- ior ) generic Linux syscall
  Push args so last-pushed maps to first kernel register (rdi).
  write(fd, buf, count): count buf fd 3 1 syscall
  NOTE: x86-64 syscall numbers differ from i386.
  Up to 6 args (rdi, rsi, rdx, r10, r8, r9).
  see also: syscall read write openr openw close

\ --- File I/O (boot — always available) ---

fstat ( buf fd -- ior ) get file status by fd
  buf must be >= 144 bytes (struct stat).
access ( mode addr -- ior ) check file permissions
  addr NUL-terminated.  mode: 0=F_OK, 1=X, 2=W, 4=R.
dup2 ( newfd oldfd -- fd ) duplicate file descriptor
fcntl2 ( arg cmd fd -- ior ) file control
pipe ( pipefd -- ior ) create pipe
  pipefd: 8-byte buffer receiving 2 ints (read fd, write fd).
ioctl3 ( arg req fd -- ior ) device control
lstat ( buf path -- ior ) like stat; doesn't follow symlinks
  path NUL-terminated.  buf >= 144 bytes.
truncate ( len fd -- ior ) truncate open file to length
flock ( op fd -- ior ) advisory file locking
  op: 1=LOCK_SH, 2=LOCK_EX, 4=LOCK_UN, 8=LOCK_NB (| to combine).
link ( new old -- ior ) create hard link (both NUL-terminated)
symlink ( new old -- ior ) create symbolic link
readlink ( len buf path -- n ) read symbolic link target
  Returns bytes placed in buf, or negative errno.
fchmod ( mode fd -- ior ) change file mode via fd
fchown ( gid uid fd -- ior ) change file owner/group via fd
umask ( mask -- prev ) set file creation mask
getdents64 ( count buf fd -- n ) read directory entries (raw)
tell ( fd -- offset ) current file position
  Convenience: lseek(fd, 0, SEEK_CUR).
  see also: fstat access dup2 fcntl2 pipe ioctl3 lstat truncate
  see also: flock link symlink readlink fchmod fchown umask
  see also: getdents64 tell read openr openw close lseek stat

\ --- Memory management (boot) ---

PROT_READ   ( -- 1 )  mmap/mprotect permission flags
PROT_WRITE  ( -- 2 )
PROT_EXEC   ( -- 4 )
MAP_SHARED  ( -- 1 )  mmap mapping flags
MAP_PRIVATE ( -- 2 )
MAP_ANONYMOUS ( -- $20 )
mmap ( off fd flags prot len addr -- ptr ) map memory
  0 -1 MAP_PRIVATE MAP_ANONYMOUS | PROT_READ PROT_WRITE | $1000 0 mmap
munmap ( len addr -- ior ) unmap memory
mprotect ( prot len addr -- ior ) set memory protection
brk ( addr -- newbrk ) change data segment size; 0 brk = query
  see also: mmap munmap mprotect brk PROT_READ MAP_ANONYMOUS

\ --- Process control (boot) ---

fork ( -- pid ) create child process
  Returns 0 in child, child pid in parent, negative on error.
execve ( envp argv filename -- ior ) execute program
wait4 ( rusage options status pid -- pid ) wait for process
  pid=-1 for any child.  options: 1=WNOHANG.
exit_group ( status -- ) exit all threads
alarm ( seconds -- prev ) set signal alarm timer; 0 cancels
setpgid ( pgid pid -- ior ) set process group ID
getpgrp ( -- pgid ) get process group ID
getpriority ( who which -- prio ) get scheduling priority
  which: 0=PRIO_PROCESS, 1=PRIO_PGRP, 2=PRIO_USER.
setpriority ( prio who which -- ior ) set scheduling priority
chroot ( path -- ior ) change root directory (NUL-terminated)
wait ( -- pid ) wait for any child
  Convenience: wait4(-1, buf, 0, 0).
  see also: fork execve wait4 exit_group alarm setpgid getpgrp
  see also: getpriority setpriority chroot wait getpid getppid

\ --- Directory / filesystem (boot) ---

getcwd ( size buf -- len ) get current working directory
mkdir ( mode addr -- ior ) create directory (NUL-terminated)
rmdir ( addr -- ior ) remove directory (NUL-terminated)
unlink ( addr -- ior ) delete file (NUL-terminated)
rename ( newpath oldpath -- ior ) rename (both NUL-terminated)
  see also: getcwd chdir mkdir rmdir unlink rename

\ --- Time and misc (boot) ---

uname ( buf -- ior ) get system information
  buf >= 390 bytes (struct utsname).
gettimeofday ( tz tv -- ior ) get time
  tv: 16-byte struct (sec + usec).  tz usually 0.
getrandom ( flags len buf -- n ) get random bytes
  flags: 0=blocking, 1=GRND_NONBLOCK, 2=GRND_RANDOM.
nanosleep ( rem req -- ior ) sleep with nanosecond precision
  req: 16-byte struct (8B seconds + 8B nanoseconds).
  rem: remaining time if interrupted (or use 0).
time ( tloc -- time ) seconds since epoch
  tloc: address to store result, or 0 for return value only.
times ( buf -- ticks ) get process times
  buf: 32-byte struct tms.
  see also: uname gettimeofday getrandom nanosleep time times

\ === Networking library (lib/64/net.ff) ===
\ Load with: needs net.ff

socket ( proto type domain -- fd ) create socket
  0 SOCK_STREAM AF_INET socket
connect ( addrlen addr fd -- ior ) connect to address
bind ( addrlen addr fd -- ior ) bind socket to address
listen ( backlog fd -- ior ) listen for connections
accept4 ( flags addrlen addr fd -- newfd ) accept connection
shutdown ( how fd -- ior ) shutdown socket
  how: SHUT_RD(0), SHUT_WR(1), SHUT_RDWR(2).
sendto ( tolen to flags len buf fd -- n ) send data
recvfrom ( fromlen from flags len buf fd -- n ) receive data
send ( flags len buf fd -- n ) send without address
recv ( flags len buf fd -- n ) receive without address
socketpair ( sv proto type domain -- ior ) create socket pair
  sv: 2-cell buffer for returned fds.
setsockopt ( optlen optval optname level fd -- ior ) set option
getsockopt ( optlen optval optname level fd -- ior ) get option
getsockname ( addrlen addr fd -- ior ) get local address
getpeername ( addrlen addr fd -- ior ) get remote address
pselect6 ( sigmask timeout exceptfds writefds readfds nfds -- n )
  Synchronous I/O multiplexing.
htons ( n -- n ) byte-swap 16-bit value for network byte order
  see also: socket connect bind listen accept4 shutdown
  see also: sendto recvfrom send recv socketpair htons

Constants: AF_UNIX(1) AF_INET(2) AF_INET6(10)
  SOCK_STREAM(1) SOCK_DGRAM(2) SOCK_RAW(3)
  SOL_SOCKET(1) SO_REUSEADDR(2) SO_KEEPALIVE(9)
  IPPROTO_IP(0) IPPROTO_TCP(6) IPPROTO_UDP(17)
  INADDR_ANY(0) INADDR_LOOPBACK($100007F)
  SHUT_RD(0) SHUT_WR(1) SHUT_RDWR(2)

\ === File test library (lib/64/stat.ff) ===
\ Load with: needs stat.ff
\ Subtraction sets ZF; drop and RET preserve it.
\ 0= just stores cond_jmp — no runtime code.
\ Caller pattern: TEST 0= IF ... THEN drop

?stat ( addr -- ior ) stat a NUL-terminated path into shared buffer
?lstat ( addr -- ior ) lstat into shared buffer (doesn't follow symlinks)
st@ ( -- mode ) read st_mode from shared buffer
sz@ ( -- size ) read st_size from shared buffer
mt@ ( -- time ) read st_mtime from shared buffer
nl@ ( -- nlink ) read st_nlink from shared buffer
  see also: ?stat ?lstat st@ sz@ mt@ nl@

fmode ( addr len -- mode|-1 ) stat path, return st_mode
fsize ( addr len -- size|-1 ) stat path, return st_size
flmode ( addr len -- mode|-1 ) lstat path, return st_mode
  Inline strings work directly: "path" fmode
  see also: fmode fsize flmode

is-reg  ( mode -- mode ) test for regular file (sets ZF)
is-dir  ( mode -- mode ) test for directory (sets ZF)
is-link ( mode -- mode ) test for symbolic link (sets ZF)
is-fifo ( mode -- mode ) test for named pipe (sets ZF)
is-sock ( mode -- mode ) test for socket (sets ZF)
is-blk  ( mode -- mode ) test for block device (sets ZF)
is-chr  ( mode -- mode ) test for character device (sets ZF)
  Chainable: "f" fmode is-reg 0= IF ... THEN drop
  see also: is-reg is-dir is-link is-fifo is-sock is-blk is-chr

can-r ( mode -- mode ) test owner-read permission (sets ZF)
can-w ( mode -- mode ) test owner-write permission (sets ZF)
can-x ( mode -- mode ) test owner-execute permission (sets ZF)
  see also: can-r can-w can-x is-reg

file? ( addr len -- ) is path a regular file? (sets ZF)
dir?  ( addr len -- ) is path a directory? (sets ZF)
link? ( addr len -- ) is path a symbolic link? (sets ZF)
  "path" dir? 0= IF ."yes" THEN
  see also: file? dir? link? fmode fsize

st.mode ( -- 24 ) offset of st_mode in struct stat
st.size ( -- 48 ) offset of st_size in struct stat
st.nlink ( -- 16 ) offset of st_nlink
st.uid  ( -- 28 ) offset of st_uid
st.gid  ( -- 32 ) offset of st_gid
st.atime ( -- 72 ) offset of st_atime
st.mtime ( -- 88 ) offset of st_mtime
st.ctime ( -- 104 ) offset of st_ctime
  see also: st.mode st.size st.nlink stat fstat lstat
